ing_Period/P%27%C3%AAng_Ting-ch%27iu\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/P&#39;\u00eang Ting-ch&#39;iu\">P'\u00eang Ting-ch'iu</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367644\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367644\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3649345</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 2 \u2014 P&#39;\u00eang Sun-y\u00fc</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">Tu Lien-ch\u00ea</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"616\" data-page-number=\"616\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/14\" data-page-index=\"14\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/14\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;14\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>P'\u00caNG</b> Sun-y\u00fc \u5f6d\u5b6b\u9079 (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u99ff\u5b6b,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u7fa8\u9580), 1631\u20131700, scholar, calligrapher, and official, was a native of Hai-yen, Chekiang. He was made a <i>chin-shih</i> in 1659, and twenty years later was summoned to compete in the special examination known as <i>po-hs\u00fceh hung-tz'\u016d</i> \u535a\u5b78\u5b8f\u8a5e which was held on April 11, 1679. He took first honors among the fifty candidates who passed the examination. A total of 188 scholars had been summoned to take it. Of this number thirty-six declined, pleaded illness, or were prevented by death from competing; and 102 failed. Of the fifty who passed, twenty-three were from Kiangsu, thirteen from Chekiang, five from Chihli, three from Anhwei, two from Kiangsi, and one each from Shensi, Honan, Shantung, and Hupei.\n</p><p>After several promotions P'\u00eang Sun-y\u00fc rose in 1688 to a sub-chancellorship in the Grand Secretariat, and in the following spring was especially commissioned to pay sacrificial honors at the tomb of Confucius in Shantung. Like all of the scholars who passed the <i>po-hs\u00fceh hung-tz'\u016d</i> examination of 1679, he had a share in the compilation of the History of the Ming Dynasty (<i>Ming-shih</i>), assisting also in the preparation of the official account of the suppression of the Sanfan Rebellion (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wu_San-kuei\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wu San-kuei\">Wu San-kuei</a></span>), entitled <i>P'ing-ting San-ni fang-l\u00fceh</i> (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Han_T%27an\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Han T&#39;an\">Han T'an</a></span>). For a time he was Director of the State Historiographer's Office (\u570b\u53f2\u9928). He retired from official life in 1697. When, two years later, Emperor Sh\u00eang-tsu was on his third tour of the South a tablet, inscribed by the imperial hand, was bestowed upon P'\u00eang as a token of extraordinary favor. The inscription, reading <i>Sung-kuei t'ang</i> \u677e\u6842\u5802 (The Hall of the Pine and the Cassia), appears in the title of his collected works, <i>Sung-kuei t'ang ch'uan chi</i> (\u5168\u96c6). This work, in 37 ch\u00fcan, was printed by his son, P'\u00eang Ching-ts\u00eang \u5f6d\u666f\u66fe in 1743. As a poet the natne of P'\u00eang Sun-y\u00fc was linked with that of his great contemporary, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wang_Shih-ch%C3%AAn\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wang Shih-ch\u00ean\">Wang Shih-ch\u00ean</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]. He was especially praised for his <i>tz'\u016d</i> or poems in irregular metre.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/489/14b; 3/59/31a; 20/1/00 (portrait); 29/2/9b; 32/2/1a; <i>Hai-yen hsien chih</i> (1877) 16/8b; <i>Ss\u016d-k'u</i> 173/5a.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Tu Lien-ch\u00ea</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Han T'an"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/P'\u00eang Sun-i"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/P'\u00eang Ting-ch'iu"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wang Shih-ch\u00ean"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wu San-kuei"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Tu Lien-ch\u00ea"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 2\n | onlysection = p'eng sun-yu\n | previous = P'\u00eang Sun-i\n | next     = P'\u00eang Ting-ch'iu\n | from     = 14\n | to       = 14\n | notes    =\n | section_author = Tu Lien-ch\u00ea\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:P'\u00eang Sun-y\u00fc}}"}}}